/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A spirometer measures the volume of air a person moves in and out.
   - Four primary volumes: tidal volume VT is one quiet breath, about 0.5 L; inspiratory reserve volume IRV is extra air after a quiet breath in, about 3.0 L; expiratory reserve volume ERV is extra air after a quiet breath out, about 1.1 L; resi
   - Capacities: IC = VT + IRV; VC = IRV + VT + ERV; FRC = ERV + RV; TLC = VC + RV.
   - Four designs are common.
   - A 3 L calibration syringe is pushed through before a session.
   - Lung gas is at 37 °C, at atmospheric pressure, and saturated with water vapour; this is BTPS.
   - PB is barometric pressure in mmHg; Pw is the saturated water vapour pressure at the spirometer temperature t (22.4 mmHg at 24 °C, 23.8 mmHg at 25 °C); 47 mmHg is the vapour pressure at 37 °C; 310 K is body temperature.
   - The forced vital capacity (FVC) manoeuvre is the standard test.
   - FEV1 is the volume exhaled in the first second.
   - An obstructive pattern (asthma, COPD) has low FEV1 and a ratio below 0.70, because narrowed airways slow the air out; FVC may be normal or low.
*/
