/* book exam traps: Using FEV1 alone: a low FEV1 occurs in both obstruction and restriction. The FEV1/FVC ratio separates the two patterns, and a true restriction still needs a low TLC to confirm it. | Expecting a spirometer to measure residual volume or FRC: that air never leaves the lungs, so only helium dilution, nitrogen washout or a plethysmograph can find it (Chapter 2.8). | Forgetting BTPS: comparing an ATPS volume with a BTPS predicted value underreads the lung volume by about 8%. | Unit slip: PEFR is often in L/min while FEV1 and FVC are in litres; divide by 60 to get L/s. | Calling any low FVC restrictive: obstruction can also reduce FVC; check the ratio and the flow-volume shape. */
