/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The pneumotachometer is a deliberate resistance in the breathing path.
   - ΔP is the pressure drop in cm H₂O; Rpt is the resistance of the pneumotachograph head in cm H₂O per L/s, its calibration factor; Q is the flow in L/s.
   - A Fleisch pneumotachograph uses many parallel capillaries: the parallel paths keep the total resistance low and the flow in each tube laminar.
   - Intra-alveolar pressure (Palv) is the pressure inside the air sacs.
   - Intrapleural pressure (Ppl) is the pressure in the thin space between lung and chest wall.
   - Ptp is transpulmonary pressure, Palv intra-alveolar pressure and Ppl intrapleural pressure, all in cm H₂O and all measured from the same reference.
   - Airway resistance (Raw) links flow and the pressure that drives it.
   - The differential transducer measures only the small drop across the head, often under 2 cm H₂O, so it needs good sensitivity and a stable zero.
   - V is volume in L; Q is flow in L/s; t is time in s.
   - Integration accumulates any offset: a zero error of 0.01 L/s for 60 s adds 0.6 L of false volume.
*/
