/* book exam traps: Mixing pressure references: airway and pneumotachometer pressures are gauge pressures, measured from atmosphere. A difference such as transpulmonary pressure is the same in any reference, but subtracting a gauge value from an absolute one gives nonsense. | Sign convention: with expiratory flow positive, Palv = Pmouth + Raw × Q; inspiratory flow reverses the sign. A sign slip turns −1 cm H₂O into +1 cm H₂O. | Linear only while laminar: Rpt is invalid once flow becomes turbulent, so high-flow readings need a linearisation curve or a larger head. | Unit slip: 1 cm H₂O is about 0.098 kPa, not 1 kPa, and about 0.74 mmHg, not 1 mmHg. | Intra-alveolar is not intrapleural: alveolar pressure is inside the air sac and swings around zero; intrapleural pressure is around the lung and stays below atmospheric in quiet breathing. */
